1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,349
You check out the commit. You run
the evaluation. You get 0.80.

2
00:00:09,689 --> 00:00:14,589
The commit is intact. Nothing was rewritten.
The tracker didn't lie either: the run

3
00:00:14,629 --> 00:00:19,528
really was started at that commit. But the
run was made from a working tree with an

4
00:00:19,568 --> 00:00:23,159
uncommitted change, and the
tracker recorded only the commit.

5
00:00:23,199 --> 00:00:27,443
Two different code states shared one
commit ID, and one of them is gone.

6
00:00:27,784 --> 00:00:33,145
By the end of this video you can say
exactly what a run record has to contain so

7
00:00:33,185 --> 00:00:38,614
that this can't happen, and you'll see a
past result reproduced from its recorded

8
00:00:38,654 --> 00:00:42,193
identifiers. Remember the
two numbers, 0.90 and 0.80.

9
00:00:42,233 --> 00:00:45,028
You'll watch one commit carry two results.

10
00:00:46,868 --> 00:00:50,698
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:50,738 --> 00:00:54,822
Three words first. A commit is one
saved snapshot of the project.

12
00:00:54,862 --> 00:00:58,438
The working tree is the folder
of files you edit and run.

13
00:00:58,478 --> 00:01:03,576
And a run is one execution of a training
or an evaluation, whose result a tracker

14
00:01:03,616 --> 00:01:03,894
logs.

15
00:01:04,234 --> 00:01:08,821
Video 175 dealt with the left column of
the chapter's table: what is in Git.

16
00:01:08,861 --> 00:01:12,108
Today is the right column,
and the arrow between them.

17
00:01:12,148 --> 00:01:16,796
Data and weights aren't in Git, and Git
still has to say which data and which

18
00:01:16,836 --> 00:01:18,136
weights a commit used.

19
00:01:18,476 --> 00:01:23,540
Two halves. First, versioning by reference:
a pointer in Git, bytes elsewhere.

20
00:01:23,580 --> 00:01:28,775
You know one instance from video 97, Git
LFS. Here you see the bare pattern, and

21
00:01:28,815 --> 00:01:34,272
then the real tools as variations of it.
Second, reproducibility: the list of things

22
00:01:34,312 --> 00:01:37,937
that identify a run, of which
the commit ID is only one.

23
00:01:38,277 --> 00:01:42,836
As in video 175, the tools of the field,
DVC, MLflow and the others, aren't

24
00:01:42,876 --> 00:01:47,682
installed in the lab. The pointer mechanism
and the run recorder you'll see are

25
00:01:47,722 --> 00:01:51,545
stand-ins written for the lab
with the Python standard library.

26
00:01:51,585 --> 00:01:56,329
Commands of the real tools are shown from
their documentation, without output.

27
00:01:58,169 --> 00:01:59,747
Here's what you'll walk away with.

28
00:02:00,087 --> 00:02:01,500
After this video you can:

29
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:05,621
Version a dataset by committing a
pointer and keeping the bytes elsewhere.

30
00:02:05,961 --> 00:02:11,065
Say what Git LFS, DVC and a hand-made pointer
have in common and where they differ.

31
00:02:11,405 --> 00:02:14,689
List what a run record must
contain besides the commit ID.

32
00:02:15,029 --> 00:02:18,282
Detect a dirty or untracked
state at run time and record it.

33
00:02:18,622 --> 00:02:22,041
Reproduce a past result from
its recorded identifiers.

34
00:02:23,881 --> 00:02:28,765
Versioning by reference. You version a
large file by committing a small file that

35
00:02:28,805 --> 00:02:33,810
names it by checksum, and by keeping the
bytes in a store that is addressed by that

36
00:02:33,850 --> 00:02:38,247
checksum. A checksum is a fingerprint
computed from every byte of a file.

37
00:02:38,287 --> 00:02:43,110
Identical bytes always give the same one,
and one changed byte gives a different

38
00:02:43,150 --> 00:02:43,353
one.

39
00:02:43,693 --> 00:02:48,771
Every tool in this area is a variation on
one pattern, which you met as Git LFS:

40
00:02:49,111 --> 00:02:51,396
Compute a cryptographic hash of the file.

41
00:02:51,736 --> 00:02:55,780
Store the file outside Git under that
hash: content-addressed storage.

42
00:02:56,120 --> 00:02:59,288
Commit a small pointer that
records the hash and the size.

43
00:02:59,628 --> 00:03:04,331
Tell Git to ignore the real file, or
let a filter swap pointer and file.

44
00:03:04,671 --> 00:03:09,665
Git then versions the pointer. git log,
git diff, branches, tags and pull requests

45
00:03:09,705 --> 00:03:14,146
all work on it, and "which data did
this commit use" has an exact answer.

46
00:03:14,486 --> 00:03:19,133
The central weakness. The pointer and the
file can disagree, and Git won't tell you.

47
00:03:19,173 --> 00:03:22,872
Git doesn't watch an ignored file.
You'll see that in the terminal.

48
00:03:23,212 --> 00:03:24,102
The real tools.

49
00:03:24,442 --> 00:03:28,576
The table is from the course's research
report, with its dates and flags.

50
00:03:28,916 --> 00:03:31,151
Two flags are carried from the report.

51
00:03:31,491 --> 00:03:37,248
The Hugging Face "all repositories
migrated" claim dates from October 2025 and

52
00:03:37,288 --> 00:03:43,490
wasn't independently verified for 2026. And
no statement of DVC's roadmap was found.

53
00:03:43,830 --> 00:03:47,600
What they have in common: a
hash, a store, a pointer in Git.

54
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:52,490
Where they differ: how the second step
happens when you move between commits.

55
00:03:52,530 --> 00:03:57,507
With the hand-made pointer and with DVC,
going back is two steps: Git moves the

56
00:03:57,547 --> 00:04:02,587
pointer, the tool moves the bytes. Git
LFS hides the second step inside a smudge

57
00:04:02,627 --> 00:04:07,921
filter, which is more convenient, and is
why a clone without the LFS client silently

58
00:04:07,961 --> 00:04:08,810
gets pointers.

59
00:04:09,150 --> 00:04:14,517
The DVC guide says of itself that DVC "is
technically not a version control system":

60
00:04:14,557 --> 00:04:17,285
it manipulates metafiles
that Git versions.

61
00:04:17,625 --> 00:04:22,012
Pinning a model from a hub. A model ID
such as org/name names whatever the

62
00:04:22,052 --> 00:04:26,498
publisher's default branch points at
today. The Hugging Face download guide

63
00:04:26,538 --> 00:04:31,344
documents a revision parameter that accepts
a branch, a tag or a commit hash, and

64
00:04:31,384 --> 00:04:36,368
says a commit hash must be the full-length
hash, not a seven-character abbreviation.

65
00:04:36,708 --> 00:04:41,613
A branch or tag in revision is a moving
reference, exactly as a Git branch is.

66
00:04:41,653 --> 00:04:46,684
Commit the hash you evaluated. That line
of configuration is the model's pointer

67
00:04:46,724 --> 00:04:51,882
file. Registry aliases such as champion
are mutable by design, which is what makes

68
00:04:51,922 --> 00:04:56,636
promotion a one-step operation and what
makes an alias useless as a record.

69
00:04:56,976 --> 00:05:01,837
Deploy by alias if you like. Log the resolved
version number and the Git commit of

70
00:05:01,877 --> 00:05:03,510
the training code beside it.

71
00:05:03,850 --> 00:05:08,933
Reproducibility. A result is identified by
the commit, the state of the working tree

72
00:05:08,973 --> 00:05:14,056
relative to that commit, the data and model
versions, the resolved configuration and

73
00:05:14,096 --> 00:05:18,020
the environment. Record all of
them or you have recorded a guess.

74
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:23,103
The tools capture less than people assume.
Picture one tracked file, edited and not

75
00:05:23,143 --> 00:05:27,599
committed: the working tree has the new
version, and the last commit does not.

76
00:05:27,639 --> 00:05:32,094
A tree in that state is called dirty.
The textbook gives three tools, from the

77
00:05:32,134 --> 00:05:32,498
report.

78
00:05:32,838 --> 00:05:37,516
MLflow: the classic run context sets the
commit, the branch and the repository URL

79
00:05:37,556 --> 00:05:42,003
when the entry point is inside a Git
repository, and doesn't inspect or record

80
00:05:42,043 --> 00:05:46,779
uncommitted changes. A newer, opt-in function
marked experimental records the dirty

81
00:05:46,819 --> 00:05:47,872
state and the diff.

82
00:05:48,212 --> 00:05:53,061
Weights and Biases: it generates a patch of
uncommitted changes when code is logged,

83
00:05:53,101 --> 00:05:56,145
but code saving is disabled
by default for all teams.

84
00:05:56,485 --> 00:06:01,568
Hydra: it writes the resolved configuration
and the command-line overrides into a

85
00:06:01,608 --> 00:06:03,528
.hydra directory for every run.

86
00:06:03,868 --> 00:06:08,139
Four caveats are attached to these
statements, and they stay attached.

87
00:06:08,179 --> 00:06:13,251
The report flags a conflict about MLflow's
branch tag: the documentation table says

88
00:06:13,291 --> 00:06:18,178
it is set for MLflow Projects only, the
source code sets it for any run inside a

89
00:06:18,218 --> 00:06:21,873
repository, and the source is
the more reliable description.

90
00:06:22,213 --> 00:06:27,399
The opt-in function is described from
source code, not from a documentation page.

91
00:06:27,439 --> 00:06:32,755
W&B's exact default for commit capture when
code saving is off wasn't tested, and a

92
00:06:32,795 --> 00:06:38,045
bug report about missing patch files exists,
so verify capture instead of assuming

93
00:06:38,085 --> 00:06:43,272
it. Hydra's behavior was read from the
development documentation, not re-verified

94
00:06:43,312 --> 00:06:44,691
against release 1.3.7.

95
00:06:45,031 --> 00:06:49,653
The consequence, in the report's words:
with classic tracking, two different code

96
00:06:49,693 --> 00:06:51,438
states can share one commit ID.

97
00:06:51,778 --> 00:06:58,617
Lock files, digests, resolved configuration.
Three rules from section 28.8.

98
00:06:58,957 --> 00:07:04,494
Lock files are committed. A lock file lists
the exact version of every dependency.

99
00:07:04,534 --> 00:07:10,138
A dependency range is resolved to exact
versions at install time, so two installs a

100
00:07:10,178 --> 00:07:15,783
month apart differ. uv, version 0.12.21,
documents uv.lock as a cross-platform lock

101
00:07:15,823 --> 00:07:21,292
file that should be checked into version
control, and --locked as the option that

102
00:07:21,332 --> 00:07:25,917
raises an error instead of updating
a lock file that is out of date.

103
00:07:26,257 --> 00:07:31,181
A lock file is generated, and it's still
committed, because it's an input to every

104
00:07:31,221 --> 00:07:36,267
later build. The test for "generated files
do not belong in Git" is whether the file

105
00:07:36,307 --> 00:07:39,355
can be regenerated identically
from what is in Git.

106
00:07:39,395 --> 00:07:42,503
A lock file cannot, since
the package index changes.

107
00:07:42,843 --> 00:07:48,133
Container images are pinned by digest. An
image tag is a mutable reference, like a

108
00:07:48,173 --> 00:07:53,529
branch. The parallel with Git is exact: tag
is to digest as branch is to commit ID.

109
00:07:53,869 --> 00:07:58,998
Configuration is recorded resolved. A
configuration built from defaults, files,

110
00:07:59,038 --> 00:08:04,298
environment variables and command-line
overrides exists in its final form only at

111
00:08:04,338 --> 00:08:04,887
run time.

112
00:08:05,227 --> 00:08:09,771
And one consequence for CI: versions
derived from tags need the tags.

113
00:08:09,811 --> 00:08:14,754
git describe names a commit by the most
recent reachable annotated tag, and

114
00:08:14,794 --> 00:08:18,275
packaging plugins derive the
package version from it.

115
00:08:18,315 --> 00:08:23,789
The default actions/checkout fetches one
commit and no tags, so the derived version

116
00:08:23,829 --> 00:08:28,107
fails or falls back. Set fetch-depth:
0 in the jobs that need it.

117
00:08:28,447 --> 00:08:33,040
Read it as three columns: the identifier,
the reference that can move, and the one

118
00:08:33,080 --> 00:08:33,719
that cannot.

119
00:08:34,059 --> 00:08:39,719
Quick quiz. Which of these may go into a
run record as the identity of the model?

120
00:08:39,759 --> 00:08:44,926
A, the model name. B, the registry
alias champion. C, the hub commit hash.

121
00:08:44,966 --> 00:08:45,770
Your answer?

122
00:08:50,610 --> 00:08:53,929
C. A name and an alias can point
at something else tomorrow.

123
00:08:53,969 --> 00:08:58,015
The commit hash was in the right-hand
column of the table: it can't move.

124
00:08:59,855 --> 00:09:04,372
Two pictures, one for each half. For the
pointer, the textbook's analogy is a

125
00:09:04,412 --> 00:09:07,924
coat-check ticket. The ticket
is small and travels with you.

126
00:09:07,964 --> 00:09:12,540
The coat stays in the cloakroom. The
analogy breaks in two places that matter.

127
00:09:12,580 --> 00:09:17,335
This ticket is derived from the coat: it's
a hash of the contents, so a different

128
00:09:17,375 --> 00:09:22,307
coat can never be handed back for it. And
a ticket is worthless if the cloakroom has

129
00:09:22,347 --> 00:09:23,076
been emptied.

130
00:09:23,416 --> 00:09:28,375
For reproducibility, a chain of custody.
A sealed evidence bag with a label is

131
00:09:28,415 --> 00:09:33,309
evidence. An open bag with the same label
is not. The commit ID is the label.

132
00:09:33,349 --> 00:09:38,372
The dirty flag says whether the bag was
open. Where it breaks: unlike a seal, a

133
00:09:38,412 --> 00:09:42,665
dirty tree can be reconstructed
exactly if you also keep the patch.

134
00:09:43,005 --> 00:09:47,489
Put the two together and you have the
sentence for the CTO: every row of the last

135
00:09:47,529 --> 00:09:51,344
table has a left column that can
move and a right column that cannot.

136
00:09:51,384 --> 00:09:53,969
A record made of left-column
values is a guess.

137
00:09:55,809 --> 00:10:00,753
Look at the bottom line: the real file is
in the working tree, ignored, and it's the

138
00:10:00,793 --> 00:10:05,619
tool, not Git, that checks it against the
pointer. Now the ignore rule: everything

139
00:10:05,659 --> 00:10:08,408
under data is ignored
except the pointer files.

140
00:10:10,248 --> 00:10:14,289
Into the lab. The tool here is the
pattern in about a hundred lines of

141
00:10:14,329 --> 00:10:18,720
standard-library Python, and the store
is a directory beside the repository.

142
00:10:19,060 --> 00:10:24,528
Step 1: add a data set by reference. 🟢
SAFE for the Git side: the commit adds a

143
00:10:24,568 --> 00:10:25,783
five-line pointer.

144
00:10:26,123 --> 00:10:30,949
Predict: after the tool has run, which
path will git status show as untracked?

145
00:10:30,989 --> 00:10:31,947
Say it out loud.

146
00:10:36,787 --> 00:10:41,953
The data file was ignored before and stays
ignored: the two exclamation marks in the

147
00:10:41,993 --> 00:10:47,034
first status. What becomes visible to Git
is the pointer: five lines of JSON for a

148
00:10:47,074 --> 00:10:48,087
file of any size.

149
00:10:48,427 --> 00:10:52,541
Step 2: a new version of the data.
Four labelled tickets arrive.

150
00:10:52,581 --> 00:10:56,630
Predict what git status prints
after the data file has changed.

151
00:11:01,470 --> 00:11:06,081
Read the first two commands together.
Four rows were appended, and git status

152
00:11:06,121 --> 00:11:09,706
printed nothing. The tool's own
verify reports the mismatch.

153
00:11:09,746 --> 00:11:14,781
After the second add, the new version of
the data is a two-line diff of the pointer:

154
00:11:14,821 --> 00:11:15,626
hash and size.

155
00:11:18,966 --> 00:11:24,863
Step 3: going back. 🟢 SAFE: git switch
--detach moves HEAD and rewrites tracked

156
00:11:24,903 --> 00:11:28,169
files, and refuses to
overwrite local edits.

157
00:11:28,509 --> 00:11:33,412
Git moved the pointer. The working file
still held the newer data until the tool

158
00:11:33,452 --> 00:11:36,501
restored the version the
pointer names. Two steps.

159
00:11:36,841 --> 00:11:38,933
Step 4: a clone, and the store.

160
00:11:39,273 --> 00:11:44,009
A clone has pointers, not data. One
checkout of the tool fetches the bytes and

161
00:11:44,049 --> 00:11:47,927
verify says ok. Now predict what
happens when the store is gone.

162
00:11:52,767 --> 00:11:57,865
A pointer whose object is gone identifies
a data set precisely and can't produce it.

163
00:11:57,905 --> 00:12:02,819
The store needs what the repository has:
backups, access control, and a retention

164
00:12:02,859 --> 00:12:06,611
rule that never deletes an object
some commit still points to.

165
00:12:06,951 --> 00:12:09,693
Step 5: a function that records the state.

166
00:12:10,033 --> 00:12:15,829
Four Git commands do the work, all 🟢 SAFE.
git rev-parse HEAD gives the commit.

167
00:12:15,869 --> 00:12:21,961
git status --porcelain=v1 -z gives a stable,
machine-readable list of what differs.

168
00:12:22,001 --> 00:12:26,468
Entries starting with two question
marks are untracked files.

169
00:12:26,508 --> 00:12:32,599
git diff HEAD --binary is the complete
uncommitted change to tracked files, and its

170
00:12:32,639 --> 00:12:38,214
SHA-256 identifies that change. git
describe --always --dirty --tags gives a

171
00:12:38,254 --> 00:12:39,692
human-readable name.

172
00:12:40,032 --> 00:12:46,131
Try it now, for thirty seconds. In any
repository of yours, type git describe

173
00:12:46,171 --> 00:12:52,350
--always --dirty --tags. It only reads.
Look at the end of the name it prints.

174
00:12:52,390 --> 00:12:53,147
I'll wait.

175
00:12:57,987 --> 00:13:03,159
If the name ends in the word dirty, a tracked
file differs from the last commit, and

176
00:13:03,199 --> 00:13:07,130
a run started now wouldn't be the
code that the commit ID names.

177
00:13:07,170 --> 00:13:11,350
If it does not, check for untracked
files as well. Step 8 shows why.

178
00:13:11,690 --> 00:13:13,578
Step 6: a recorded run.

179
00:13:13,918 --> 00:13:17,919
Five identifiers in one file: the
commit aa18ad2 with dirty: false.

180
00:13:17,959 --> 00:13:22,924
The checksum of the data actually read,
which you can compare with the pointer from

181
00:13:22,964 --> 00:13:26,603
step 1. A hash of the lock file.
A hash of the configuration.

182
00:13:26,643 --> 00:13:31,247
And the resolved configuration itself,
so that the record can be read without

183
00:13:31,287 --> 00:13:32,574
checking anything out.

184
00:13:35,914 --> 00:13:40,985
Step 7: two results, one commit. Try an idea
without committing it: one more keyword

185
00:13:41,025 --> 00:13:42,932
in the evaluation configuration.

186
00:13:43,272 --> 00:13:46,558
The script refuses a tracked
run from a dirty tree.

187
00:13:46,598 --> 00:13:50,666
That's the first defensible policy.
With --allow-dirty it runs.

188
00:13:50,706 --> 00:13:53,797
Predict: what distinguishes
the two run records?

189
00:13:58,637 --> 00:14:02,133
Accuracy 0.8 and accuracy
0.9, both at commit aa18ad2.

190
00:14:02,173 --> 00:14:05,931
There are the two numbers from
the opening, on one commit.

191
00:14:05,971 --> 00:14:11,432
A tracker that stores only the commit shows
two runs of the same code with different

192
00:14:11,472 --> 00:14:16,868
results. This record tells them apart:
dirty: true, a hash of the uncommitted diff,

193
00:14:16,908 --> 00:14:22,303
and the diff itself saved as uncommitted.patch.
The last command proves the hash is

194
00:14:22,343 --> 00:14:27,608
of that diff. That's the second defensible
policy: if dirty runs are allowed, log

195
00:14:27,648 --> 00:14:30,031
the status and the diff as artifacts.

196
00:14:30,371 --> 00:14:35,366
The root-cause box of section 28.7 sums
it up: the tracker recorded HEAD, and not

197
00:14:35,406 --> 00:14:37,914
the difference between HEAD and what ran.

198
00:14:38,254 --> 00:14:41,124
Step 8: untracked files are a third state.

199
00:14:41,464 --> 00:14:46,283
dirty is false, because no tracked file
changed, and git diff HEAD is empty.

200
00:14:46,323 --> 00:14:49,863
Yet a new module on the import
path can change behavior.

201
00:14:49,903 --> 00:14:55,105
The function reports untracked files
separately, and --require-clean refuses both.

202
00:14:55,145 --> 00:15:00,412
According to the report's reading of its
source, the MLflow opt-in function doesn't

203
00:15:00,452 --> 00:15:02,394
count untracked files as dirty.

204
00:15:02,734 --> 00:15:05,799
Step 9: today's number is
not the recorded number.

205
00:15:06,139 --> 00:15:12,601
Today, on main: 0.7857 on 14
examples. The tracker says 0.8 on 10.

206
00:15:12,641 --> 00:15:15,458
Don't guess. Read the record.

207
00:15:15,798 --> 00:15:20,583
The record names a commit, says the tree
was clean, and names the data by checksum.

208
00:15:20,923 --> 00:15:26,439
Step 10: reproduce beside your work. 🟢
SAFE: git worktree add --detach creates a

209
00:15:26,479 --> 00:15:29,772
second working tree and
doesn't touch the first.

210
00:15:30,112 --> 00:15:34,953
The worktree is at the recorded commit. The
data tool restores the bytes the pointer

211
00:15:34,993 --> 00:15:39,544
at that commit names, and the checksum
in the pointer equals the one in the run

212
00:15:39,584 --> 00:15:39,951
record.

213
00:15:40,291 --> 00:15:45,958
0.8000 on 10 examples, and the new run
record is identical to the recorded one.

214
00:15:45,998 --> 00:15:51,520
That's what "reproduced" means: the same
identifiers in, the same record out.

215
00:15:53,360 --> 00:15:55,008
Five mistakes to watch for.

216
00:15:55,348 --> 00:16:00,349
Recording only the commit ID of a run. Root
cause: git rev-parse HEAD names the last

217
00:16:00,389 --> 00:16:04,971
commit and says nothing about the index,
the working tree or untracked files.

218
00:16:05,311 --> 00:16:10,008
Changing the data file and forgetting the
pointer. Root cause: the data file is

219
00:16:10,048 --> 00:16:14,984
ignored, so Git does not watch it, and only
the tool's verify step compares it with

220
00:16:15,024 --> 00:16:15,704
the pointer.

221
00:16:16,044 --> 00:16:20,854
Treating the pointer as the backup. Root
cause: the pointer identifies the bytes and

222
00:16:20,894 --> 00:16:24,723
cannot produce them; the store is
a dependency that can be emptied.

223
00:16:25,063 --> 00:16:28,566
Recording a model name, a
registry alias or an image tag.

224
00:16:28,606 --> 00:16:33,725
Root cause: each is a mutable reference,
like a branch, and can name something else

225
00:16:33,765 --> 00:16:34,285
tomorrow.

226
00:16:34,625 --> 00:16:39,731
Ignoring the lock file because it is generated.
Root cause: it cannot be regenerated

227
00:16:39,771 --> 00:16:44,877
identically from what is in Git, since the
package index changes, so it is an input.

228
00:16:46,717 --> 00:16:51,651
Now, out of the lab. An evaluation team
reports a ranking metric to a customer every

229
00:16:51,691 --> 00:16:56,566
quarter. A new engineer is asked to reproduce
last quarter's number and cannot: the

230
00:16:56,606 --> 00:17:00,356
tracker names a commit, and the
commit gives a different result.

231
00:17:00,696 --> 00:17:03,966
The lead goes down the table
of identifiers in order.

232
00:17:04,006 --> 00:17:09,151
Code: the commit exists. The tracker has
no dirty flag, so the state of the tree is

233
00:17:09,191 --> 00:17:13,212
unknown. Dependencies: the repository
has a lock file, committed.

234
00:17:13,252 --> 00:17:18,085
Data: the path is recorded, not a checksum,
and the file at that path has been

235
00:17:18,125 --> 00:17:19,397
replaced twice since.

236
00:17:19,737 --> 00:17:24,164
Model: recorded as a registry alias,
which has been promoted once since.

237
00:17:24,204 --> 00:17:27,514
Three of the rows were recorded
as mutable references.

238
00:17:27,554 --> 00:17:32,416
The honest report to the customer is the
one the root-cause box prescribes: the

239
00:17:32,456 --> 00:17:37,442
result can't be reproduced. Here's why.
Here's the rerun from a commit with every

240
00:17:37,482 --> 00:17:38,683
identifier recorded.

241
00:17:39,023 --> 00:17:43,943
The team then changes the evaluation entry
point. It refuses to start a tracked run

242
00:17:43,983 --> 00:17:48,964
from a tree that is dirty or has untracked
files, and it writes the commit, the data

243
00:17:49,004 --> 00:17:53,984
checksum from the pointer, the resolved
model version and the resolved configuration

244
00:17:54,024 --> 00:17:55,179
into the run record.

245
00:17:57,019 --> 00:18:02,771
Your turn. Do Lab 33.2, "Version
a dataset by reference", in

246
00:18:02,811 --> 00:18:06,054
lab-manual/m33-ai-ml-workflows.md.

247
00:18:06,394 --> 00:18:11,134
Before you change the data file in the
lab, predict what git status --short will

248
00:18:11,174 --> 00:18:16,153
print and what the tool's verify step will
print. Before the lab's failure scenario,

249
00:18:16,193 --> 00:18:20,873
write down how a pointer and a file can
come to disagree without any error, and

250
00:18:20,913 --> 00:18:25,713
which command would have shown it. The
lab's questions are answered in a separate

251
00:18:25,753 --> 00:18:27,206
file. Attempt them first.

252
00:18:27,546 --> 00:18:33,941
The challenge is Exercise 33.7, Level
5, "The number in the paper cannot be

253
00:18:33,981 --> 00:18:37,888
reproduced", in
exercises/m32-m34-practice.md.

254
00:18:41,728 --> 00:18:46,443
Answer out loud. The question asks for three
things per cause: the cause, its place

255
00:18:46,483 --> 00:18:51,255
in an order, and its evidence. A strong
answer uses the rows of the identifier table

256
00:18:51,295 --> 00:18:55,380
as its list, so that nothing is
forgotten: code state beyond the commit,

257
00:18:55,420 --> 00:18:58,474
dependencies, environment,
data, model, configuration.

258
00:18:58,814 --> 00:19:03,516
It justifies the order, for example by
how cheap each test is and how often each

259
00:19:03,556 --> 00:19:08,377
cause occurs. For each cause it names the
record or the command that would confirm

260
00:19:08,417 --> 00:19:13,179
or exclude it. And it says plainly what
you report when the evidence needed for a

261
00:19:13,219 --> 00:19:14,660
cause was never recorded.

262
00:19:16,500 --> 00:19:18,550
Let's land this, in your own words.

263
00:19:18,890 --> 00:19:23,300
A large file is versioned by committing a
pointer that names it by checksum, with

264
00:19:23,340 --> 00:19:25,443
the bytes in a content-addressed store.

265
00:19:25,783 --> 00:19:30,983
Git LFS, DVC and a hand-made pointer share
hash, store and pointer; they differ in

266
00:19:31,023 --> 00:19:33,794
how the bytes follow
when the pointer moves.

267
00:19:34,134 --> 00:19:38,429
The pointer and the file can disagree
without Git noticing, and the store can be

268
00:19:38,469 --> 00:19:38,700
lost.

269
00:19:39,040 --> 00:19:43,833
A run is identified by the commit, the
dirty state and diff, untracked files, the

270
00:19:43,873 --> 00:19:48,129
data and model versions, the resolved
configuration and the environment.

271
00:19:48,469 --> 00:19:53,710
Record immutable identifiers: full
commit ID, lock file, image digest, data

272
00:19:53,750 --> 00:19:58,428
checksum, model version or hub
commit hash, resolved configuration.

273
00:20:00,268 --> 00:20:07,813
Read sections 28.6 to 28.8. Do Lab 33.3,
"Reproduce a past result from its recorded

274
00:20:07,853 --> 00:20:15,398
identifiers", in lab-manual/m33-ai-ml-workflows.md,
including its failure scenario,

275
00:20:15,438 --> 00:20:22,527
which this video didn't show. Then do
Exercise 33.4, Level 3, "Three runs, one

276
00:20:22,567 --> 00:20:29,747
commit ID", and Exercise 33.5, Level 3,
"The pointer and the file disagree", in

277
00:20:29,787 --> 00:20:32,488
exercises/m32-m34-practice.md.

278
00:20:32,828 --> 00:20:37,909
Today you versioned data without putting it
in Git, and you reproduced a result from

279
00:20:37,949 --> 00:20:43,031
its record, to the last digit. If the list
of identifiers feels long, that's normal:

280
00:20:43,071 --> 00:20:46,384
the table has six rows, and
you can keep it beside you.

281
00:20:46,424 --> 00:20:51,505
Next time: gitignore and gitattributes for
ML projects, pre-commit backed by CI, and

282
00:20:51,545 --> 00:20:56,504
a professional AI project repository. Until
then, look at the state first and type

283
00:20:56,544 --> 00:20:58,455
second. See you in the next one.
